                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ




Don Chino’s bodega has an awning the color of faded orange soda and
a speaker over the door, and Don Chino himself stood under it with his
arms crossed like a bouncer at a very small club. The domino players
had stopped slapping tiles, and everybody was waiting for someone
else to say the thing.
   Rafa stopped under the speaker and closed his eyes.
   “How flat?” he said.
   “Like a table. A table with nothing on it.”
   “And it comes back?”
   “Every eight seconds.”
   He bought a Malta Goya. Don Chino rang it up without looking at
either of us. “They fixed my freezer, mijo,” he said, to me, to nobody. I
hadn’t said a word. “Fourteen years I called the landlord about that
freezer. These people came in January, free speaker, free Wi-Fi, and a
guy fixed the freezer in a day. A day.” He pushed the change across the
counter. “Nobody fixes my freezer.”
   Nobody had to sneak anything into Don Chino’s bodega. They fixed
his freezer.
   The barbershop was next. Papo and Wilson have shared a mirror for
twenty years and an argument about the Mets bullpen so old it’s be‐
come a form of marriage. I’d been getting my hair cut there since I was
eleven, and in all that time I’d never once heard it quiet. Barbershops
are supposed to be the loudest rooms on earth. That’s the whole point.
You go for the fade and stay for the verdict.
   That night the two of them clipped side by side with their backs to
each other, not arguing. A kid sat in the chair with half a lineup, wait‐
ing. Nobody had said a word in a while. The speaker in the corner
played something soft with a pressed place under it. On Friday the
block had been fighting over a lottery ticket. Outside, the loop made
people mean. Inside, it made them go out like a pilot light.
   “It’s not one thing,” Rafa said, on the sidewalk, quietly. “Mean out
here. Quiet in there. It depends on the room.” He looked at his hands.
“It always depends on the room.”



